The images and the installer of a chalkos release also contain third-party free software, such
as the Linux kernel, systemd and the GNU C Library, each under its own licence: among others the
GNU GPL, the GNU LGPL and the MIT licence. Each release publishes a software bill of materials
(SBOM) of each image, the release asset <image>.spdx.json, which lists the image's packages with
their licences and the URLs of their source.

The release's flake.lock fixes the corresponding source of every component, including the
patches that nixpkgs applies, and Nix fetches it; cache.nixos.org and tarballs.nixos.org mirror
it. For the release vX.Y.Z:

  nix flake archive github:trevex/chalkos/vX.Y.Z
    copies chalkos's source and every flake input to the Nix store, with nixpkgs, whose
    expressions hold the build instructions and patches of each component.

  nix build --inputs-from github:trevex/chalkos/vX.Y.Z nixpkgs#util-linux.src
    fetches the upstream source of one component, here util-linux, from the nixpkgs that the
    release locks.

For three years after each release, the project also gives anyone who asks the complete
corresponding source of that release's third-party components, for no more than the cost of
physically performing the distribution. Ask for it in an issue of
https://github.com/trevex/chalkos/issues.
